🧩 What is an API in PHP?

API stands for Application Programming Interface — it’s a way for two software systems to communicate with each other.

In PHP, APIs are often used to:

Get data from another server or service

Send data to another application (for example, to create a new user, send a message, or place an order)

💡 Simple Definition

An API is a set of rules that allows one program to talk to another.
In web development, APIs usually send and receive data over HTTP, often in JSON format.

🌍 Example: Get Weather Data Using PHP

Here’s a simple example of using a public API to get real-time weather information:

<?php
$response = file_get_contents("https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast?latitude=35.6892&longitude=51.3890&current_weather=true");
$data = json_decode($response, true);

echo "Temperature in Tehran: " . $data['current_weather']['temperature'] . " °C";
?>


Explanation:

https://api.open-meteo.com/... → this is the API endpoint

PHP sends an HTTP request to the API

The API responds with JSON data

We decode it and display the temperature

⚙️ Common Types of APIs in PHP

REST API — most popular, uses HTTP + JSON

SOAP API — older, uses XML

GraphQL API — modern, flexible query language

🔧 Common PHP Tools for Working with APIs

file_get_contents() → simple, good for basic GET requests

cURL → more advanced, supports headers, POST, etc.

